{"id":1178,"date":"2020-11-23T14:12:53","date_gmt":"2020-11-23T22:12:53","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1178"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:25:21","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:25:21","slug":"codrone-arduino-controller-leds","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-controller-leds\/","title":{"rendered":"Controller LEDs"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cb02\" name=\"6a2215d33cb02\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Inputs vs. outputs<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The CoDrone controller has 8 built-in blue LEDs that can be programmed. If you look at the Rokit Smart Inventor Board map, these LEDs each share pins 11 through 18 with the bottom digital IR sensors, but you can switch between using the pins as sensors or as LEDs. To do this, you need to use different pin modes.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;963&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The digital IR sensors should be written as inputs, which means they read information. With CoDrone Pro, this usually looks like you covering a sensor with your finger so it&#8217;s triggered. Using the IR sensors as inputs looks like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">pinMode(11,INPUT)<\/pre>\n<p>The LEDs should be written as outputs, which means information is sent from them. With CoDrone Pro, this usually looks like LEDs flashing or buzzers making noise. Using the LEDs as outputs usually looks like this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">pinMode(11,OUTPUT)<\/pre>\n<p>A pin cannot be an input and output at the same time, so you need to make the decision about which pins you want to be IR sensors (inputs) or LEDs (outputs). By default, the CoDrone sets pins 11, 12, 14, 15, 17, and 18 as inputs, while pins 13 and 16 are outputs.<\/p>\n<p>One weird thing: middle IR sensor pins 14 and 15 are connected to the same sensor, so <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalRead(14)<\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalRead(15)<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>will always give the same answer. However, on the LED side, pins 14 and 15 are connected to two separate LEDs, so <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalWrite(14, HIGH)<\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalWrite(15,HIGH)<\/code> will control different LEDs.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1179&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cc35\" name=\"6a2215d33cc35\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Digital write<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you want to turn your LEDs on (or off!), you need to use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">digitalWrite()<\/code> function. This is what it will look like in your code:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(pin number, output)<\/pre>\n<p>What all of that means:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The pin number\u00a0is the number that&#8217;s attached to the LED<\/li>\n<li>Output is what you want the LED to do. It can either be HIGH (on) or LOW (off), or it can be 1 (on) or 0 (off).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>HIGH and LOW refer to the voltage of the pin. HIgh will set the pin to high voltage, which will turn the LED on, and low will set the pin to low voltage, which will turn it off. Again, you can also use 1 (on) or 0 (off). An LED will stay in its current state unless it&#8217;s told otherwise.<\/p>\n<p>Try this program to turn your LED on and off!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup() {\r\n  pinMode(11, OUTPUT);\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  digitalWrite(11, HIGH);\r\n  delay(2000);\r\n  digitalWrite(11, LOW);\r\n  delay(2000);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Set up pin 11 as an output.<\/li>\n<li>Turn LED 11 on for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>Turn LED 11 off for 2 seconds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33ccd7\" name=\"6a2215d33ccd7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Programming LEDs with flags<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]One of the ways you can program your remote control&#8217;s LEDs is with flags! In this program, they&#8217;re named <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Mode<\/code> instead, but again, you can name your flag variable whatever you want. LEDs 13 and 16 are programmed as outputs and sensor 18 is programmed as an input, so when sensor 18 is triggered, LEDs 13 and 16 will do something. You can repeatedly cover sensor 18 to see all of the different combinations!<\/p>\n<p>Warning: you need to connect to a CoDrone for this program in order for it to work, but it will not take off or fly.<\/p>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror notranslate cm-s-default\">\n<div>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nint Mode;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n  digitalWrite(13, LOW);\r\n  digitalWrite(16, LOW);\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n\r\n  if (bt8) {\r\n    Mode = Mode + 1;\r\n\r\n    if (Mode &gt; 3) {\r\n      Mode = 0;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    if ( Mode == 0) {\r\n      digitalWrite(13, LOW);\r\n      digitalWrite(16, LOW);\r\n    }\r\n    else if ( Mode == 1) {\r\n      digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\r\n      digitalWrite(16, LOW);\r\n    }\r\n    else if ( Mode == 2) {\r\n      digitalWrite(13, LOW);\r\n      digitalWrite(16, HIGH);\r\n    }\r\n    else if ( Mode == 3) {\r\n      digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\r\n      digitalWrite(16, HIGH);\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.Buzz(3000 + (Mode * 2000), 7);\r\n    delay(400); \r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Establish Mode as a variable.<\/li>\n<li>Begin communication at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a nearby drone.<\/li>\n<li>Set pins 13 and 16 as outputs and turn them off.<\/li>\n<li>Set pin 18 as an input.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 18 is covered, add a value of 1 to Mode.<\/li>\n<li>If Mode is greater than 3, set Mode to 0.<\/li>\n<li>If Mode is 0, turn LEDs 13 and 16 off.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if Mode is 1, turn LED 13 on and LED 16 off.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if Mode is 2, turn LED 13 off and LED 16 on.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if Mode is 3, turn LEDs 13 and 16 on.<\/li>\n<li>Play a note on the buzzer.<\/li>\n<li>Wait 400 milliseconds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cd75\" name=\"6a2215d33cd75\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Switching pin modes<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There might be times when you can&#8217;t decide whether to use a pin as an LED or IR sensor. You can use one pin as both an input and output in the same program. All you need to do is switch to input right before your sensor read and switch back to output right after. It should look something like this, and you&#8217;ll need to set it up at the beginning of void loop:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">pinMode(18, INPUT); \r\nbyte bt8 = digitalRead(18); \r\npinMode(18, OUTPUT);<\/pre>\n<p>Look at the block of code right at the beginning of void loop to see what we mean. Warning: you need to connect to a CoDrone for this program in order for it to work, but it will not take off or fly.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nint Flag;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n  CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n  CoDrone.pair();\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  pinMode(18, INPUT);\r\n  byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n  pinMode(18, OUTPUT);\r\n\r\n  if (bt8) {\r\n    if (Flag == 0) {\r\n      Flag = 1; \r\n    } \r\n    else {\r\n      Flag = 0;\r\n    } \r\n\r\n    CoDrone.Buzz(4000 + (Flag * 2000), 7);\r\n    delay(400);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  if (Flag == 1) {\r\n    digitalWrite(18, HIGH);\r\n  }\r\n  else if (Flag == 0) {\r\n    digitalWrite(18, LOW);\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Set <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Flag<\/code> as a variable.<\/li>\n<li>Establish communication at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a nearby drone.<\/li>\n<li>Set pin 18 as an input and sensor.<\/li>\n<li>Set pin 18 as an output.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 18 is triggered and if the flag is 0, set the flag to 1.<\/li>\n<li>Else, set the flag to 0,<\/li>\n<li>Play a sound on the buzzer for 400 milliseconds.<\/li>\n<li>If <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Flag<\/code> is at 1, turn LED 18 on.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Flag<\/code> is at 0, turn LED 18 off.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Usually LED commands are in the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">if (bt8)<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>\u00a0if statement, but in this case, they should be outside of that if statement. This is because you&#8217;re switching between input and output with the same sensor, so the high or low commands need to be sent continuously.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cde3\" name=\"6a2215d33cde3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing intensity with Pulse Width Modulation<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]An LED&#8217;s brightness, or intensity, can be changed by quickly turning the LED on and off. The Smart Inventor Board switches the LED on and off faster than your eyes can notice, so all you&#8217;ll see is a decrease in brightness. For example, if you wanted your LED to be at full brightness, you could just leave it on the entire time. However, if you wanted your LED to be on at less than half of the brightness, you would need to leave it on less than half of the time. To do this, you would need to use Pulse Width Modulation (PWM).<\/p>\n<p>Really quick side note: in this case, a pulse is a quick switch from off to on and then back to off again. If you did this with your LED, it would flick on and off once, and it would happen so fast that you probably wouldn&#8217;t see it.<\/p>\n<p>If you want to decrease the brightness of your LEDs to less than half, you&#8217;ll need more off time in between each of the pulses. Make sure there isn&#8217;t too much off time since your eyes will actually notice that the LEDs are off instead of just appearing dim. Check the image below to see what that looks like![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1180&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Not sure how much time to leave in between each pulse? The start of one pulse to the start of another is about 20 milliseconds. If you wanted your LEDs to be on for less than half of the brightness, you would set the LEDs to be on for less than 10 milliseconds. Check the image to see what very low intensity looks like, since you&#8217;ll be using that for your example code![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1181&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33ce60\" name=\"6a2215d33ce60\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Dim LED example code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Try the code below for dim LEDs!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nunsigned long SetTime;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  if (micros() - SetTime &lt; 100) {\r\n    digitalWrite(13, HIGH);\r\n  }\r\n  else if (micros() - SetTime &lt; 20000) {\r\n    digitalWrite(13, LOW);\r\n  }\r\n  else {\r\n    SetTime = micros();\r\n  }\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Create an unsigned long variable for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>If current microseconds minus the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> is less than 100 microseconds, turn LED 13 on.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if current microseconds minus the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> is less than 20,000 microseconds, turn LED 13 off.<\/li>\n<li>Else, <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> gets the current microseconds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Wondering why this code is using microseconds? Because you&#8217;re working with pulses that can only go up to 20 milliseconds, microseconds are much more accurate.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cf11\" name=\"6a2215d33cf11\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Joystick control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Pulse width is the amount of time that the pulse is on. For example, if you have a 10 millisecond pulse, your pulse width is also 10 milliseconds. You can set your pulse width to a variable like the one below so you can do more with it!<\/p>\n<div class=\"CodeMirror notranslate cm-s-default\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">if (micros() - SetTime &lt; PulseWidth)<\/pre>\n<p>For example, you can use your left joystick to control how bright or dim your LEDs are with this code.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nunsigned long SetTime;\r\nunsigned long PulseWidth;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n  int LeftJoystick = CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(23)); \r\n  PulseWidth = (LeftJoystick + 100)*50;\r\n\r\n  if (micros() - SetTime &lt; PulseWidth) {\r\n    digitalWrite(13, HIGH); \r\n  }\r\n  else if (micros() - SetTime &lt; 20000) {\r\n    digitalWrite(13, LOW);\r\n  } \r\n  else {\r\n    SetTime = micros(); \r\n  } \r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Create unsigned long variables for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">PulseWidth<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Create an int variable for <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">LeftJoystick<\/code> that will read and get its value.<\/li>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">PulseWidth<\/code> will get the value of 50 times the left joystick plus 100.<\/li>\n<li>If the current microseconds minus <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> is less than the value of <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">PulseWidth<\/code>, turn LED 13 on.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if the current microseconds minus <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> is less than the value of 20,000, turn LED 13 off.<\/li>\n<li>Else, <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">SetTime<\/code> will get the current microseconds.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Here&#8217;s more of a translation with some review!<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The code line <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(A23))<\/code> is used in our controller code, which you learned about in lesson Analog Read and Controller Flight. \u00a0This line reads your joystick\u2019s analog inputs and gives it a re-scaled number from -100 to 100.<\/li>\n<li>In the pulse width equation <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">PulseWidth = (LeftJoystick + 100)*50<\/code>, you&#8217;re adding 100 to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">LeftJoystick<\/code> so you don\u2019t get a negative number. Your range is now 0 to 200.<\/li>\n<li>The number that you get from that part of the equation is then multiplied by 50 so your new range is 0 to 10,000 microseconds. This means that with the joystick all the way up, your LED&#8217;s intensity will be 50%, and all the way down will be 0%.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d33cf8b\" name=\"6a2215d33cf8b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p><strong>Challenge: Shiny Objects<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create an interactive menu using your controller\u2019s LEDs and IR sensors!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Each menu option should light up its own LED.<\/li>\n<li>Each menu option should have its own unique function, like changing the CoDrone\u2019s LED color, flying in a specific direction, different controller flight controls&#8230;you get it!<\/li>\n<li>The left IR sensor should change your menu option by -1, or move your LED to the left.<\/li>\n<li>The right IR sensor should change your menu option by +1, or move your LED to the right.<\/li>\n<li>You must include a kill switch!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Inputs vs. outputs [vc_column_text]The CoDrone controller has 8 built-in blue LEDs that can be programmed. If you look at the Rokit Smart Inventor Board map, these LEDs&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-controller-leds\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1749,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1815,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2163,2164,2165,2120,2122,2111],"class_list":["post-1178","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-led","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-remote-control","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1178","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1178\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1182,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1178\/revisions\/1182"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1749"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1178"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1178"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1178"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}